# B-tree and B+ tree indexes

## Why not a binary tree

Disk and SSD reads come in pages (8 KiB in Postgres). A node with hundreds of keys per page keeps the tree 3–4 levels deep even for hundreds of millions of rows.

## B+ tree specifics

- all values (or row pointers) live in the **leaves**; internal nodes hold only separator keys;
- leaves are linked left to right, so a range scan finds the start and then walks the leaf chain;
- fan-out is high because internal nodes are small.

## Insert and split

Insert into the correct leaf. If it overflows, split it into two half-full leaves, copy the first key of the right leaf up into the parent as a new separator. If the parent overflows, split it too and **push** the middle key up. A split of the root adds a level; that is the only way the tree grows taller, so it stays balanced.

## Delete

Remove from the leaf; if it underflows, borrow from a sibling or merge with it and remove a separator from the parent. Many real systems skip merging and just let pages be sparse.

## Practical

- Composite index (a, b) helps `WHERE a = ?` and `WHERE a = ? AND b > ?`, not `WHERE b = ?` alone.
- Covering / index-only scans avoid heap lookups (Postgres needs the visibility map).
- Fill factor < 100 leaves room for inserts and reduces splits.
